<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Tabriz</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1799 CE (approx.) · capital Tabriz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The khanate of the great city of Tabriz in the turbulent half-century after Nader Shah, ruled for much of it by the Kurdish Donboli family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, the chief city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, came under local khans after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747, as rivals including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azad-khan-afghan">Azad Khan Afghan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-khan-afshar">Fath Ali Khan Afshar</a> fought over the region. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf-qoli-khan-donboli">Najaf Qoli Khan</a> of the Donboli family was confirmed as governor and ruled largely independently.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf-qoli-khan-donboli">Najaf Qoli Khan</a> rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> after the devastating earthquake of 1780, constructing new walls and gates. The city remained a major centre of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, famous for its bazaar and crafts. The khans balanced between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zands</a>, the Donbolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoy">Khoy</a> and other neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in the 1790s, and from 1799 the city became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> crown prince, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>, as governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-tabriz.jpg" alt="Khanate of Tabriz" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Sword313 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabriz_Khanate_(eng).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1799 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tabriz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Najaf Qoli Khan Donboli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tabriz Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=khanate-of-tabriz" aria-label="Khanate of Tabriz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5833 809 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Tabriz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6153 1065L6298 1144M6153 1065L6298 1144M6153 1065L6364 1269"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6298 1144h0M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tabriz. <a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=khanate-of-tabriz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
